<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-facial-recognition">
  <title>Comparing software for facial recognition</title>

  <introduction>
    <blockquote>
      <p>"Here's looking at you, kid."</p>
      <p>--- Humphrey Bogart to Ingrid Bergman in <em>Casablanca</em></p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      <em>Background.</em> A key study evaluated how three facial-recognition systems performed across people with
      different skin shades.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Questions.</em> How do error rates vary by sex and skin colour? Are the patterns similar across the three
      software systems?
    </p>
    <p><em>Sources.</em> Buolamwini and Gebru study data and results.</p>
    <p>
      <em>Structure.</em> Prediction counts for three software systems across 1,270 individuals and six skin-shade
      groups.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-face-software">
    <title>How well do the softwares do?</title>

    <p>
      The study dataset was balanced by sex and skin type, and the task was binary sex classification from images.
      Published results showed much higher error rates for dark-skinned females than for light-skinned males.
    </p>

    <p>
      Summary counts were reconstructed from published percentages under integer-count constraints.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-facebar">
      <image source="images/faceBar-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Numbers of different skin tones in the dataset for both sexes.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Group sizes are broadly similar, though males are more numerous overall, especially in selected skin-tone
      groups.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-faceh">
      <image source="images/faceh-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          Error rates by sex and skin colour for the three software systems, shown side by side.
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-face3">
      <image source="images/face3-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          Error rates by sex and skin colour for each software system, stacked vertically for detailed comparison.
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Error rates are generally low for males, higher for females, and rise with darker skin shades. Microsoft tends
      to perform best of the three, though all systems show substantial weaknesses in the hardest groups.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-face-ppv">
    <title>Are positive predictive values (PPV) a good criterion?</title>

    <p>
      Many binary-performance criteria are possible. Because PPV values are often high, plotting FDR = (1 - PPV) can
      be more informative.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-facefdr">
      <image source="images/faceFDR-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          FDRs by sex and skin colour for three software systems, with males on the left and females on the right.
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The FDR display shows low overall rates but also important structural differences. Prediction totals by sex can
      differ from study totals, and darker skin groups still show the highest rates.
    </p>

    <p>
      Across multiple criteria, the same substantive conclusion remains: the systems perform unevenly and worst on
      dark-skinned females.
    </p>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        <em>Answers.</em> All three software packages had higher error rates for females than for males, and error
        rates rose as skin shade darkened.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Further questions.</em> With individual-level records, it would be informative to compare system
        predictions case by case rather than only in grouped summaries.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-face-takeaways">
      <title>Graphical takeaways</title>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Graphics communicate overall pattern differences more effectively than percentage tables.</p></li>
        <li><p>Doubledecker plots show why error-rate comparisons can be clearer than PPV comparisons.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </subsection>
  </section>
</chapter>
